# Copy to `.env.signing` (gitignored) and fill in the block for the platform you
# are building on. Loaded automatically by electron/signing.config.cjs, so a
# signed build is still just `npm run desktop:dist`.
#
# This file is for SECRETS. The non-secret half of Windows signing — the Azure
# account and certificate profile names — lives in the REPLACE ME block at the
# top of electron/signing.config.cjs, though every value there can also be set
# here if you would rather keep it out of the repository.
#
# Leave a section commented out and that platform builds unsigned. Nothing here
# is required to build. See DESKTOP.md § Signing for what each option costs and
# what it buys you.
#
# ⚠ Never commit `.env.signing`, the .pfx/.p12 it points at, or the .p8 key.


# ─── Windows: Azure Trusted Signing ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The current option for a newly issued certificate (~$10/month, no hardware).
# These are an Entra ID service principal with the "Trusted Signing Certificate
# Profile Signer" role on your signing account.
#
# Requires PowerShell, so it only runs on a Windows machine or runner.
#
# Authentication goes through Azure's own EnvironmentCredential, which accepts
# any one of the three sets below. Set one *completely* — a set missing a
# variable is refused rather than quietly ignored, because "ignored" means an
# unsigned installer that looks like a successful build.

#AZURE_TENANT_ID=
#AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
#AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Or, instead of the secret, a client certificate:
#AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=

# Or a user account (least suited to CI):
#AZURE_USERNAME=
#AZURE_PASSWORD=

# Optional — override the REPLACE ME block in electron/signing.config.cjs.
#AZURE_PUBLISHER_NAME=CN=Your Name
#AZURE_ENDPOINT=https://wus2.codesigning.azure.net
#AZURE_CODE_SIGNING_ACCOUNT_NAME=
#AZURE_CERTIFICATE_PROFILE_NAME=


# ─── Windows: certificate file ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only works for OV certificates issued before the June 2023 CA/Browser Forum
# rules, or for an EV certificate whose token exports a .pfx — newer OV
# certificates are hardware- or HSM-bound and cannot be handed over as a file.
#
# WIN_CSC_LINK takes an absolute path to the .pfx, or its base64 contents (which
# is what you want in CI). The unprefixed CSC_LINK works too, but it is shared
# with macOS; the WIN_ prefix keeps the two apart on a machine that does both.

#WIN_CSC_LINK=
#WIN_CSC_KEY_PASSWORD=


# ─── macOS: Developer ID certificate ─────────────────────────────────────────
# A "Developer ID Application" certificate from the Apple Developer Program
# ($99/year), exported as .p12. Path or base64, same as above.

#CSC_LINK=
#CSC_KEY_PASSWORD=

# On a Mac that has a Developer ID in its keychain, electron-builder finds and
# uses it even with nothing set above. Uncomment to force a genuinely unsigned
# build — useful when you want to reproduce what a contributor without
# certificates gets.
#CSC_IDENTITY_AUTO_DISCOVERY=false


# ─── macOS: notarization ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Signing alone is not enough: Gatekeeper wants the app stapled by Apple.
# Notarization runs automatically as soon as one of these credential sets is
# present — there is no flag to turn it on.
#
# Preferred: an App Store Connect API key. APPLE_API_KEY is a PATH to the .p8
# file, not its contents (a common half-hour). Note that Apple lets you download
# a given .p8 exactly once.

#APPLE_API_KEY=/absolute/path/to/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
#APPLE_API_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX
#APPLE_API_ISSUER=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Alternative: an Apple ID with an app-specific password from appleid.apple.com.
# Simpler to set up, worse in CI — it is your account password's neighbour, and
# it expires when you change the account password.
#APPLE_ID=you@example.com
#APPLE_APP_SPECIFIC_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
#APPLE_TEAM_ID=XXXXXXXXXX


# ─── Linux ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to configure. AppImages are conventionally distributed unsigned.
